  "query_preview": "(Saturday through Tuesday)...\nThe cooling trend will continue through Tuesday with high\ntemperatures on Tuesday as much as 10 to 15 degrees below average\nfor the mountains, deserts, and inland valleys. High temperatures\nfor next Tuesday will be mostly in the 70s for the coast and\nvalleys with the lower deserts in the lower to mid 80s. \n\nA closed low pressure system will develop off the California\ncoast on Thursday. This low pressure system will move toward the \nsoutheast and absorb the moisture from former Hurricane Rachel, \nthen move northeastward into southern California and northern \nBaja bringing showers for late Saturday night through Sunday with \nanother closed low pressure system from the north continuing \nchances for showers for Monday and Tuesday. \n\nNBM chances for 0.10 inch or more of rainfall through Sunday \nnight are 45 to 55 percent with chances for 0.50 inch or more \nranging from 30 percent near the coast to 40 percent for the \nmountains. Chances for 1 inch or more of rainfall range from 15 \npercent near the coast to 25 to 35 percent for the mountains.\n\nThrough Tuesday night, NBM chances for 0.25 inch or more of\nrainfall ranges from 50 percent near the coast to 55 to 65 percent\nin the mountains. Chances for 1 inch or more of rainfall ranges\nfrom around 25 percent near the coast to 35 to locally 45 percent\nin the mountains. Chances for 2 inches or more of rainfall ranges\nfrom around 5 percent near the coast to 10 to 15 percent for the\nmountains. There could be a few inches of snowfall on the very\nhighest mountain peaks above 9000 feet for Monday night and\nTuesday as snow levels lower to 8000 to 8500 feet.\n\nOnshore winds will strengthen for Sunday and Monday with southwest\nto west winds for Sunday afternoon through Monday morning gusting\nto 20 to 30 mph for the coastal waters to the valleys and to 35 to\n45 mph for the mountains and deserts with gusts to 55 to 65 mph\nfor the northern Coachella Valley below the San Gorgonio Pass.",
